{
  "id": "alignment-amoebots",
  "model": "programmable-matter",
  "name": "Alignment",
  "origin": {
    "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
    "year": 2022,
    "note": "APPROX/RANDOM 2022."
  },
  "derived_from": {
    "problem": "compression-amoebots",
    "kind": "generalisation of",
    "relation": "Alignment adds an orientation to each particle; the same chains also give compression and expansion [Kedia2022Alignment]."
  },
  "summary": "Oriented particles, following a local stochastic rule, must align their orientations.",
  "statement": "Each particle carries one of $q \\ge 2$ orientations, a spin state, not a sense of direction. Particles run a local Markov chain; the system should **align** (most particles share an orientation) or not, depending on the bias, with high probability in the stationary distribution, both when connectivity is kept and when particles may disconnect.",
  "primer": {
    "intro": "Picture a school of fish or a flock of birds in which every animal turns to face the same way as its neighbours. Here $n$ particles on the triangular grid each carry an *orientation*, an arrow pointing in one of $q \\ge 2$ directions; this arrow is just a state, not a compass, and a particle can only compare its arrow with its neighbours' arrows. A particle sits on one node of an endless triangular grid and moves in two steps: it *expands* into an empty neighbouring node, briefly occupying both, then *contracts* into one of the two; so one expansion and one contraction move it by one node. One randomly chosen particle acts at a time; it either steps or turns its arrow. The group should *align* (one direction is shared by most particles) or stay *non-aligned* (all directions about equally common), depending on a bias; this is studied both when the group must stay connected and when it may fall apart.",
    "ideas": [
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Step or turn, with a bias towards agreeing neighbours",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
        "idea": "In the connected setting there are two biases: $\\lambda$ rewards having neighbours, as in compression, and a second bias, written $\\gamma$ in [Kedia2022Alignment], rewards neighbours whose arrows agree. A randomly chosen particle does one of two things, with equal chance.\\n\\n1. **Step.** Pick a random empty neighbouring node; check locally that the group stays connected without holes; move with a probability that grows with the gain in neighbours and in agreeing neighbours.\\n2. **Turn.** Pick a new direction for its arrow; accept it with a probability that grows with the gain in agreeing neighbours (certainly, if it gains).",
        "analysis": "With both biases large (for instance $\\gamma > 29.3(q-1)$ and $\\lambda\\gamma$ large enough), the group is compressed with high probability, and under further conditions on $\\gamma$ one direction dominates [Kedia2022Alignment]. With $\\lambda$ large but $\\gamma$ small, the group is compressed but its arrows stay evenly split among the $q$ directions; with $\\lambda$ small, the group stays stretched out. Compactness matters: a long thin bottleneck lets two groups with different directions coexist cheaply, so alignment is proved only for well compressed groups.",
        "why_it_matters": "It brings the Potts and clock models of magnetism into programmable matter, with particles that move as well as turn."
      },
      {
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "When particles may drift apart: one knob",
        "variant": "stochastic",
        "ref": "Kedia2022Alignment",
        "idea": "If the group is allowed to disconnect, the connectivity check is dropped: a particle may step to any empty neighbouring node. A single bias then rewards, at the same time, having neighbours and having agreeing neighbours.\\n\\n1. Pick step or turn with equal chance.\\n2. Step: move to a random empty neighbouring node, accepted with a probability that grows with the gain in (agreeing) neighbours.\\n3. Turn: as before.",
        "analysis": "When the single bias is large, the particles gather into compact clumps and align; when it is small, they disperse and the directions stay balanced [Kedia2022Alignment]. The proof is harder than in the connected case because a spread-out system can contain many small clusters scattered over the grid.",
        "why_it_matters": "It shows that the stochastic approach does not need the connectivity rule, which is costly to check and not always realistic."
      }
    ]
  },
  "history": "Alignment brought Potts and clock models from statistical physics to programmable matter [Kedia2022Alignment].",
